\details{
\subsection{Meta fields}{

The following meta fields are available via \code{p[["..."]]} (or \code{p$...}):
\itemize{
\item \code{name} — the name of the pipeline.
\item \code{view} — the absolute row indices of the steps covered by a view, or
\code{NULL} for a full pipeline.
\item \code{pipenv} — the shared inner environment holding the pipeline's state.
All views and extracted subsets reference the same environment, so
mutations are shared. The step table is available as
\code{p[["pipenv"]][["data"]]}.
}
}

\subsection{Virtual methods}{

All pipeline functions are exposed as \emph{virtual} methods via \code{p$...}
(or \code{p[["..."]]}). For example, \code{p$add(...)} is shorthand for
\code{pip_add(p, ...)} and returns the updated pipeline, which allows
chaining: \code{p$add("s1", ...) |> p$add("s2", ...)}.
}

\subsection{Step-table columns}{

\code{p[["column"]]} returns a column of the step table, named by the step
names. For views, the column is restricted to the steps covered by the
view. Meta fields take priority over columns of the same name. The
two-index form \code{p[[row, column]]} extracts a single cell, where \code{row} is
an integer row index or a step name.
}

\itemize{
\item \code{p[[step, "step"]] <- newName} — rename the step (\code{\link[=pip_rename]{pip_rename()}});
references in dependent steps are updated as well. Assigning \code{NULL}
removes the step, together with its downstream steps
(\code{\link[=pip_remove]{pip_remove()}} with \code{force = TRUE}).
\item \code{p[[step, "fun"]] <- fun} — replace the step's function
(\code{\link[=pip_replace]{pip_replace()}}); the tags and the execution mode are kept and the
downstream steps are marked as outdated.
\item \code{p[[step, "params"]] <- list(...)} — update the step's parameters
(\code{\link[=pip_set_params]{pip_set_params()}}).
\item \code{p[[step, "tags"]] <- tags} — set the step's tags to exactly \code{tags}
(a character vector); \code{NULL} clears all tags.
\item \code{p[[step, "locked"]] <- TRUE|FALSE} — lock or unlock the step
(\code{\link[=pip_lock]{pip_lock()}} / \code{\link[=pip_unlock]{pip_unlock()}}).
\item \code{p[[step, "exec"]] <- mode} — set the step's execution mode.
\item \code{p[[step, "state"]] <- state} — set the step's state.
\item \code{p[[step, "time"]] <- time} — set the step's time stamp (a single
\code{POSIXct} value).
\item \code{p[[step, "out"]] <- value} — set the step's stored output.
}

Assigning to any other step-table column, or to a meta field other than
\code{name} and \code{view}, is not supported. To remove a step, use \code{\link[=pip_remove]{pip_remove()}}.

Views are supported as well. For a view, \code{i} is interpreted relative to the
steps covered by the view: an integer refers to the n-th visible step, and a
step name must be part of the view. Because views share the pipeline
environment, step properties are written through to the originating
pipeline, while \code{name} only renames the view itself.

If \code{value} is a \code{pipeflow} pipeline or view, the writable step properties
of its steps are copied to the selected rows: \code{p[i, ] <- q[i, ]} copies
the steps of \code{q} selected by \code{i} into the steps of \code{p} selected by \code{i}
(the rows of both sides are aligned by position and must have the same
length). In this form, \code{i} is required and \code{j} must be omitted. The
properties are copied in the order \code{step}, \code{fun}, \code{params}, \code{out},
\code{state}, \code{time}, \code{tags}, \code{locked}, \code{exec}: the step is renamed to the
source name (a no-op when it is unchanged), the function is replaced with
the source function (\code{\link[=pip_replace]{pip_replace()}}; its dependencies are recomputed
against the steps of \code{p}), the unbound parameters of the source are
applied on top of the new function defaults, and the runtime state and
annotation columns are overwritten (locked steps included). The
structural columns \code{nodeId}, \code{depends} and \code{unbound} are not copied.
Instead of \code{p[i, j] <- q}, use \code{p[i, j] <- q[i, j]} to copy the values of
a single property; the one-column table returned by the extraction form
is unwrapped and assigned element-wise.
}
